+## `Gambonanza.CrumbleApi.Crumble`
+
+The Crumble Control API, a library mod like GambitApi. It exposes the crumble
+mode the game keeps private in `CrumbleManager`. Reference
+`Gambonanza.CrumbleApi.dll`, add `"dependencies": ["CrumbleApi"]` to your
+`mod.json`, and call the static `Crumble` class from a gambit, a mod, or the
+console.
+
+Everything is safe to call before a run exists (during `OnLoad`, in the menu):
+handles are remembered and applied once the game's `CrumbleManager` is found,
+reads return zero/false, and actions are no-ops. `Crumble.IsBound` tells you
+whether the API has hooked the game yet.
+
+### The one-gambit example
+
+```csharp
+using Blukulele.CHE;
+using Gambonanza.CrumbleApi;
+
+public sealed class GambitBedrock : BaseGambit
+{
+    private CrumbleHandle _freeze;
+
+    // Start/OnDestroy is exactly the lifetime of "the player owns this card".
+    private void Start()     => _freeze = Crumble.Freeze(this);
+    private void OnDestroy() => _freeze?.Dispose();
+
+    public override void Trigger() => VisualEffect();
+}
+```
+
+Register it with `GambitBuilder.Create("bedrock")....WithBaseGambit<GambitBedrock>().Register()`
+as usual. Passing `this` as the owner means the handle is released on its own
+when Unity destroys the gambit, so a card that is sold mid-run cannot leave the
+board frozen even if `OnDestroy` never ran.
+
+### Modifiers
+
+Each returns a `CrumbleHandle` (`IDisposable`). Dispose it to undo. Handles
+from different mods stack; the console's `crumble` command lists who holds what.
+
+| Call | While the handle is alive |
+| ---- | ------------------------- |
+| `Crumble.Freeze(owner = null, label = null)` | The crumble is paused: the countdown does not tick, tiles that are shaking do not fall, and the crumble picks no new tiles. Everything resumes where it left off. Tiles the Mask boss, a Crumbler enemy or the heavy-landing strain shake outside the per-turn step still queue up and fall together on the first step after release. |
+| `Crumble.Block(owner = null, label = null)` | Crumble mode cannot begin. The countdown still ticks but stalls one turn short of the threshold. Does nothing to a crumble already under way - use `Stop()` for that. |
+| `Crumble.Delay(turns, owner = null, label = null)` | Adds `turns` to the countdown. The indicator under the board grows the extra lights and the enemy AI's "urgency" reads the longer wait. A saved run resumes against it too: the game decides "resume in crumble mode" before gambits are re-created, so when a Delay lands during a just-loaded stage and the counter is below the new threshold, the API undoes that relaunch. Delays sum, capped at `Crumble.MaxExtraTurns` (500); `Crumble.ExtraTurns` is the number that counts. `handle.Turns` is settable, so a gambit can grow its delay over a run. Use `Block()` for "never". |
+
+`owner` is any `UnityEngine.Object`; when it is destroyed the handle releases
+itself. `label` is for diagnostics and defaults to the owner's component type
+or the calling assembly.
+
+### Direct actions
+
+| Call | What it does |
+| ---- | ------------ |
+| `Crumble.Start(fireEvents = true)` | Begin crumble mode now, exactly as the game does when the countdown runs out: the counter is raised to the threshold (so a save resumes in crumble mode), `OnCrumble` fires (Scepter, Chamberlain react), the CRUMBLE build influence grows, particles and banner play. `fireEvents: false` is what the game does when resuming a save. |
+| `Crumble.Stop(resetCounter = true)` | End crumble mode: flag off, particles off, every shaking tile settles, and the countdown restarts from zero. With `resetCounter: false` the next turn will start it again (fanfare included) unless a `Block` or `Delay` is in place. |
+| `Crumble.TurnCounter = n` | Set the countdown counter; the indicator lights follow. |
+| `Crumble.ShakeTile(tile)` | Make a tile shake (the game's `CrumblerEffect`). Shaking tiles fall on the next step only while something drives the crumble - crumble mode, a Crumbler enemy, the Mask boss or the CRUMBLE_STOCK_TO_BOARD strain - otherwise they keep shaking until one of those starts, or until `CalmTile`. |
+| `Crumble.CalmTile(tile)` | Stop a tile shaking and forget it, so it will not fall. |
+| `Crumble.RestoreTile(tile)` | Bring a fallen tile back, as the game does between stages. |
+| `Crumble.ShakeNextBatch()` | Run the game's own "pick the next tiles to shake" once (2 tiles, 3 with the BIG CRUMBLE strain, from the run's seeded random). |
+| `Crumble.RefreshIndicator()` | Rebuild the indicator from the current numbers. The API calls it itself after anything it changes. |
+
+### Reads
+
+| Member | Meaning |
+| ------ | ------- |
+| `IsBound` | The API has found `CrumbleManager` and hooked its per-turn step. |
+| `IsActive` | Crumble mode is on. The game's `CrumbleMode` flag, except that a frozen crumble still reads true during the few frames the API masks the flag around the game's step (settable, but prefer `Start`/`Stop`). |
+| `TurnCounter` | Turns played this stage as the game counts them: a capture skips the next tick, and so does using Wait while a Clock enemy is on the board. |
+| `BaseTurnsToWait` | The wait the game itself uses, read from the scene once (5 in current builds). |
+| `ExtraTurns` / `TurnsToWait` | Delay total after the cap / `Base + Extra`, which is what the game reads as `TurnToWait`. |
+| `WaveBonusTurns` | The game's per-wave addition: 0 for the first five stages, then +1 per five stages up to 5. With Better AI it is (that + 1) x 1.5 rounded up, so 2 on the first five stages and up to 9. |
+| `Threshold` / `TurnsUntilCrumble` | `TurnsToWait + WaveBonusTurns` / turns left before crumble mode begins. |
+| `SkipNextIncrement` | The game's one-shot "do not tick this turn" flag (set by the game on a capture, and on Wait while a Clock enemy is on the board). Set it from `OnBeforeStep` to skip a single tick by hand. |
+| `IsFrozen` / `IsBlocked` | At least one live `Freeze` / `Block` handle exists. |
+| `CrumblerInGame` | The game's flag for a Crumbler enemy being on the board. |
+| `ShakingTiles` / `FallenTiles` | Copies of the game's private tile lists. |
+| `Handles` | Every live handle, for diagnostics. |
+| `Manager` | The live `CrumbleManager`, for anything the API does not wrap. |
+
+### Events
+
+`Crumble.OnBeforeStep` fires at the start of each player turn just before the
+game's own crumble step - the private method that ticks the counter, starts
+crumble mode, drops shaking tiles and shakes new ones. Handles are applied
+after it fires, so anything you set there (e.g. `SkipNextIncrement`) is
+honoured. `Crumble.OnAfterStep` fires right after the step.
+
+### How it hooks a private method without Harmony
+
+`CrumbleManager.Start` subscribes its private `PlayerTurnEffects` to
+`TurnManager.OnPlayerCheckIfCanPlay`, a plain multicast `Action`. Multicast
+delegates invoke in subscription order, and a delegate built with
+`Delegate.CreateDelegate` over the same target and method compares equal to the
+game's own, so the API finds it in the invocation list and rebuilds the list as
+`[before, PlayerTurnEffects, after]`. It re-checks that a few times a second
+and repairs it if anything re-subscribes. Freeze masks `CrumbleMode` and
+`CrumblerInGame` and hands the step an empty tile list for its duration; Block
+clamps the counter one short of the threshold and skips the tick; Delay rewrites
+the private `m_TurnToWait` so every reader of `TurnToWait` sees the longer
+wait. Source: `sample_mods/CrumbleApi/src/CrumbleCore.cs`.
+
+Things to know:
+
+- Tiles already shaking when a Freeze starts keep shaking and fall on the first
+  step after it ends.
+- Block does not touch a crumble that is already active, and a run saved with
+  the counter past the threshold resumes in crumble mode regardless.
+- `Manager.OnCrumble` cannot veto a crumble: the game invokes it before it
+  commits the flag. Use `Block()` to prevent one, and `OnAfterStep` (watch
+  `IsActive` go false to true) to react once it is on.
+- Disabling the CrumbleApi mod from the console restores the game's wait and
+  removes the hook; existing handles stay inert until it is re-enabled.
+- Like everything in this framework it binds to game internals by name. A game
+  update that renames one of the private fields or methods it reflects on logs
+  a warning at load and the affected parts go inert; a renamed public type or
+  member (`CrumbleManager`, `TurnManager.OnPlayerCheckIfCanPlay`, ...) still
+  breaks the API the way it breaks any mod compiled against the game.
+
